<span id="S3.T2.11.5.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:10.0pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.11.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T2.11.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">(d)</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.11.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.T2.11.5.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block" style="width:45.0pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.11.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:62.1pt;"><span id="S3.T2.11.5.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;position:relative; bottom:-0.9pt;"><img src="2509.09064v1/Figs/fig4.png" id="S3.T2.g4" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:86/86;" width="86" height="86" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"></span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.11.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.T2.11.5.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:380.0pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.11.5.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T2.11.5.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">This image is 
</span><span id="S3.T2.11.5.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#2F5496;--ltx-bg-color:#DAE3F3;">a transverse CT scan of the abdomen, offering a detailed cross-sectional view of the internal organs. The large, rounded structure in the center is likely the liver, with its distinct texture and density. Adjacent to it, the stomach and spleen are visible, with the spleen showing a brighter area indicative of different tissue or a lesion. The vertebral column is centrally located at the bottom, providing a structural reference.</span><span id="S3.T2.11.5.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> The grayscale contrast differentiates between various tissue densities, essential for identifying abnormalities. This detailed imaging is crucial for diagnosing abdominal conditions and guiding further medical evaluation and treatment.</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T2.11.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T2.11.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.T2.11.6.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:10.0pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.11.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T2.11.6.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">(e)</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.11.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.T2.11.6.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block" style="width:45.0pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.11.6.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:62.1pt;"><span id="S3.T2.11.6.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;position:relative; bottom:-0.9pt;"><img src="2509.09064v1/Figs/fig5.png" id="S3.T2.g5" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:86/86;" width="86" height="86" alt="[Uncaptioned image]"></span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S3.T2.11.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_b ltx_border_t">
<span id="S3.T2.11.6.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:380.0pt;">
<span id="S3.T2.11.6.3.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.T2.11.6.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">This image is 
</span><span id="S3.T2.11.6.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#2F5496;--ltx-bg-color:#DAE3F3;">a sagittal MRI scan</span><span id="S3.T2.11.6.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> of the 
</span><span id="S3.T2.11.6.3.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#C74744;--ltx-bg-color:#FABDC2;">abdomen</span><span id="S3.T2.11.6.3.1.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;">, showcasing various internal structures in detail. 
</span><span id="S3.T2.11.6.3.1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#C74744;--ltx-bg-color:#FABDC2;">Central to the image is a large, circular organ, likely the liver, with its characteristic texture and surrounding tissues.</span><span id="S3.T2.11.6.3.1.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> 
</span><span id="S3.T2.11.6.3.1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#2F5496;--ltx-bg-color:#DAE3F3;">The diaphragm is visible, delineating the boundary between the thoracic and abdominal cavities.</span><span id="S3.T2.11.6.3.1.1.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> 
</span><span id="S3.T2.11.6.3.1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:70%;--ltx-fg-color:#2F5496;--ltx-bg-color:#DAE3F3;">The vertebral column is seen running vertically along the left side, providing a reference point for orientation.</span><span id="S3.T2.11.6.3.1.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:70%;"> The grayscale contrast highlights different tissue densities, facilitating the diagnosis and evaluation of abdominal conditions.</span></span>
</span></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-B</span> </span><span id="S3.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Triplet Representation Learning</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our framework employs an encoder-decoder structure as its 3D visual backbone and ensures alignment with 2D image and text features via pre-trained and pre-aligned image and text encoders.
For 2D image and text encoders, we prefer those that are pre-aligned in existing vision-language models, e.g., CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>. Detailed settings are provided in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="IV-A Pretraining Setup and Settings ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Enhancing 3D Medical Image Understanding with Pretraining Aided by 2D Multimodal Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, given the dataset <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> consisting of 3D medical volumes paired with 2D image-text pairs, our goal is to learn a 3D visual representation using a 3D volume encoder <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{V}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{V}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> with the help of a pair of pre-aligned 2D image encoder <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{I}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{I}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> and text encoder <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{T}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{T}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math>.
For instance, given a sample triplet <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k}:(x_{k},s_{k}^{(i,j)},t_{k}^{(i,j)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k}:(x_{k},s_{k}^{(i,j)},t_{k}^{(i,j)})</annotation></semantics></math>, we compute their respective feature representations:
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{x}_{k}=f_{V}(x_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>x</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>V</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{x}_{k}=f_{V}(x_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> for the 3D volume <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{s}_{(i,j,k)}=f_{I}(s_{k}^{(i,j)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{s}_{(i,j,k)}=f_{I}(s_{k}^{(i,j)})</annotation></semantics></math> for the 2D image slice <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k}^{(i,j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k}^{(i,j)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and
<math id="S3.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{t}_{(i,j,k)}=f_{T}(t_{k}^{(i,j)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{t}_{(i,j,k)}=f_{T}(t_{k}^{(i,j)})</annotation></semantics></math> for the textual description <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{(i,j,k)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>t</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{(i,j,k)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Thus, for each triplet <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain its triplet feature set <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}:(h^{x}_{k},h^{s}_{(i,j,k)},h^{t}_{(i,j,k)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>x</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}:(h^{x}_{k},h^{s}_{(i,j,k)},h^{t}_{(i,j,k)})</annotation></semantics></math>. This feature set <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> encapsulates the encoded representations of the 3D medical volume, a randomly selected 2D image slice, and its corresponding textual description, respectively.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-C</span> </span><span id="S3.SS3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Triplet Alignment</span></h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the triplets <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{H_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{H_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the text features are pre-aligned with the image features of their corresponding 2D slices. However, a disparity exists between the 3D image features <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{h^{v}_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{h^{v}_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and the pairs of 2D image and text features <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{(h^{s}_{(i,j,k)},h^{t}_{(i,j,k)})\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{(h^{s}_{(i,j,k)},h^{t}_{(i,j,k)})\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Bridging this gap between 2D and 3D feature representations is crucial for enabling effective self-learning during the pretraining stage. One solution is to align 3D image features with the features of image slices and text individually.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To achieve alignment, we employ a query techinque, which is further enhanced by incorporating knowledge of the plane-slice location of the randomly selected image slice. As depicted in Fig. <a href="#S1.F1" title="Fig. 1 ‣ I Introduction ‣ Enhancing 3D Medical Image Understanding with Pretraining Aided by 2D Multimodal Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we propose a Plane-Slice Attention Transformer (PSAT) module, which adopts a collection of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> learnable query embeddings to facilitate a projection of 3D volume features into the 2D image and text embedding space of CLIP.
These queries serve as adaptive parameters that learn to decode 3D volume features into slice-specific 2D representations. Each query functions as a spatial probe, selectively attending to regions within the 3D volume that are anatomically relevant to the target 2D slice.
Specifically, these learnable queries interact with each other through a self-attention layer, then engage with the volume feature <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h^{v}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h^{v}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> via a cross-attention layer, producing an output of <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> encoded visual vectors, one for each query embedding.
These vectors are subsequently processed by a multi-layer perceptron (MLP), resulting in the projection of the volume embedding <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{h}^{v}_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>k</mi><mi>v</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{h}^{v}_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This projected embedding is then aligned with the image slice and text embeddings using an OT-based alignment method, as described in Section <a href="#S3.SS4.SSS1" title="III-D1 Alignment Loss ‣ III-D Learning Objectives ‣ III Methodology ‣ Enhancing 3D Medical Image Understanding with Pretraining Aided by 2D Multimodal Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III-D1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To inform the location of an image slice within its associated image volume, we propose incorporating the actual slice position into the query learning process. Specifically, the image slice <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k}^{(i,j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k}^{(i,j)}</annotation></semantics></math> is extracted from the image volume <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and is located at the <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math>-th slice of the <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th plane, the 3D-2D projection should be aware of this orientation relationship between image volume and slice. Therefore, we introduce a plane-slice position embedding for the volume feature to promote the model’s ability to learn orientation and geometric relationships. Specifically, we first construct the plane-slice position embedding <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times I\times J}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>I</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>J</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times I\times J}</annotation></semantics></math> with zero-initialized parameters, where <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is the embedding dimension of the model, <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of planes, and <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="J" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>J</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">J</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of slices.
During pre-training, when dealing with an image slice <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{k}^{(i,j)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>s</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{k}^{(i,j)}</annotation></semantics></math>, for instance, when a sample corresponds to the eighth slice (i.e., <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j=8</annotation></semantics></math>) of the coronal plane (i.e., <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i=0</annotation></semantics></math>), we inject the plane-slice position embedding <math id="S3.SS3.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="V_{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{c\times 1\times 1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V_{p}\in\mathbb{R}^{c\times 1\times 1}</annotation></semantics></math> for the eighth slice at the coronal plane into the volume feature and queries, following general positional encoding in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">By incorporating actual slice position into the query learning process, the model gains precise spatial awareness of each slice’s anatomical context within the 3D volume. This spatial grounding allows queries to act as informed selectors rather than generic decoders, significantly improving the fidelity of 2D representations derived from volumetric data. Such alignment is critical for leveraging powerful pre-trained 2D MLLMs in 3D medical image understanding. The improved 3D-to-2D projection serves as a robust bridge between volumetric data and text supervision, enabling effective pretraining without the need for manual annotations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S3.SS4.6" class="ltx_text">III-D</span> </span><span id="S3.SS4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Learning Objectives</span></h3>

<section id="S3.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS4.SSS1.6" class="ltx_text">III-D</span>1 </span>Alignment Loss</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Contrastive learning, as used in CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite>, offers an efficient self-learning technique by bringing the representations of paired data closer together in the embedding space while pushing apart the representations of non-paired data. However, the use of large language models inevitably leads to some noise issues, as shown in Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="TABLE II ‣ III-A Triplet Generation: 3D Image Volume, 2D Image Slice and Corresponding Textual Descriptions ‣ III Methodology ‣ Enhancing 3D Medical Image Understanding with Pretraining Aided by 2D Multimodal Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">II</span></a>(e), resulting in text descriptions that are imperfectly paired with image slices. Also, 3D image features are partially paired with those of 2D slices and text descriptions. These are challenges faced by standard contrastive learning techniques.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Optimal Transport (OT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>]</cite> studies how to move and distribute resources or mass and find the optimal way to transport mass from one distribution to another while minimizing a certain cost. When the source and target distributions are unequal, partial optimal transport (POT) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>]</cite> is a feasible solution. To address misaligned or partially aligned triplet features, we propose a variant of POT to mitigate their impact during pretraining. This approach allows for partial alignment, where only a subset of the 3D features are aligned with the 2D image and text features, demonstrating improved noise tolerance.
Specifically, due to the presence of noisy correspondences, the empirical matching <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}</annotation></semantics></math> of POT becomes corrupted compared to standard OT, leading to certain rows without a match.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We denote the incomplete optimal transport plan <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{s,\epsilon,c}^{X,Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{s,\epsilon,c}^{X,Y}</annotation></semantics></math> as:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{s,\epsilon,c}^{X,Y}=\underset{\pi\in\Pi_{s}\left(P_{X},P_{Y}\right)}{\arg\min}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\pi_{ij}c\left(x_{i},y_{j}\right)-\epsilon\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\pi_{ij}\log\pi_{ij}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mi>min</mi></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">π</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">∈</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em" mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">P</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">Y</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{s,\epsilon,c}^{X,Y}=\underset{\pi\in\Pi_{s}\left(P_{X},P_{Y}\right)}{\arg\min}\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\pi_{ij}c\left(x_{i},y_{j}\right)-\epsilon\sum_{i=1}^{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n}\pi_{ij}\log\pi_{ij},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Pi_{s}\left(P_{X},P_{Y}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">Π</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Pi_{s}\left(P_{X},P_{Y}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the set of incomplete transportation
plans or couplings between two empirical probability measures <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P_{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P_{Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is the amount of mass to be transported, <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are the pair of two samples, <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{ij}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{ij}</annotation></semantics></math> is the coupling value of <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> is the ground metric, e.g., Euclidean distance or Mahalanobis distance. When <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the optimization problem can be
solved by using a generalization of the Bregman projections algorithm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite>.
Therefore, given a mini-batch <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X^{b},S^{b},T^{b})\sim D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X^{b},S^{b},T^{b})\sim D</annotation></semantics></math>, the mini-batch POT (mPOT) solves the following optimization problem:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{mPOT}}=\mathbb{E}_{(X_{b},S_{b},T_{b})\sim D}[KL(\hat{\pi}^{b}||\pi_{s,\epsilon,c}^{X^{b},S^{b}})+KL(\hat{\pi}^{b}||\pi_{s,\epsilon,c}^{X^{b},T^{b}})]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>mPOT</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>b</mi></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>b</mi></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{mPOT}}=\mathbb{E}_{(X_{b},S_{b},T_{b})\sim D}[KL(\hat{\pi}^{b}||\pi_{s,\epsilon,c}^{X^{b},S^{b}})+KL(\hat{\pi}^{b}||\pi_{s,\epsilon,c}^{X^{b},T^{b}})].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the mini-batch size, <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{s,\epsilon,c}^{X^{b},S^{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{s,\epsilon,c}^{X^{b},S^{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{s,\epsilon,c}^{X^{b},T^{b}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{s,\epsilon,c}^{X^{b},T^{b}}</annotation></semantics></math> represent the transportation plan. These plans aim to align volume features with slice features and text features, respectively. Additionally, <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}^{b}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>b</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}^{b}</annotation></semantics></math> is a <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\times b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\times b</annotation></semantics></math> matrix such that <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{ii}=1/b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{ii}=1/b</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.SSS1.p3.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}_{ij}=0(i\neq j)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}_{ij}=0(i\neq j)</annotation></semantics></math>, ensuring uniformity within the mini-batch.
Given that embeddings from encoders may lack proper alignment in terms of scaling across dimensions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>]</cite>, we tackle this issue by substituting the commonly used Euclidean distance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a>]</cite> with the Mahalanobis distance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a>]</cite> as the ground metric.
For more information, readers can refer to the training algorithm presented in Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="In III-D1 Alignment Loss ‣ III-D Learning Objectives ‣ III Methodology ‣ Enhancing 3D Medical Image Understanding with Pretraining Aided by 2D Multimodal Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm">
<div id="alg1.3" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.3.1" class="ltx_listingline">
<span id="alg1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="alg1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> </span>Initialized 3D volume encoder <math id="alg1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{V(\theta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{V(\theta)}</annotation></semantics></math> and 3D volume projection network <math id="alg1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{Proj(\psi)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{Proj(\psi)}</annotation></semantics></math>, frozen 2D image encoder <math id="alg1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{I}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{I}</annotation></semantics></math> and text encoder <math id="alg1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, initialized interaction matrix <math id="alg1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> in the Mahalanobis distance, training data <math id="alg1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, batch size <math id="alg1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, amount of mass to be transported <math id="alg1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math>, entropy regularization coefficient <math id="alg1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> and learning rate <math id="alg1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg1.3.2.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="alg1.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> </span>Learned 3D volume encoder <math id="alg1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{V(\theta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{V(\theta)}</annotation></semantics></math>, projection network <math id="alg1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{Proj(\psi)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{Proj(\psi)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and interaction matrix <math id="alg1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.3" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg1.3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> <em id="alg1.3.3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not converged</em> <span id="alg1.3.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.4" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Draw a mini-batch from the training set: <math id="alg1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X^{b},S^{b},T^{b})=(x_{i},s_{i},t_{i})_{i=1}^{b}\sim D;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo>∼</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X^{b},S^{b},T^{b})=(x_{i},s_{i},t_{i})_{i=1}^{b}\sim D;</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.5" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Extract projected volume embedding, image slice embedding, and text embedding, respectively: 
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.6" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <math id="alg1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{H}^{X^{b}}=\{\hat{h}_{i}^{x}\}_{i=1}^{b}=\{f_{Proj(\psi)}(f_{V(\theta)}(x_{i}))\}_{i=1}^{b}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>h</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mi>x</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>b</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{H}^{X^{b}}=\{\hat{h}_{i}^{x}\}_{i=1}^{b}=\{f_{Proj(\psi)}(f_{V(\theta)}(x_{i}))\}_{i=1}^{b},</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.7" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <math id="alg1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{S^{b}}=h_{i}^{s}=\{f_{I}(s_{i})\}_{i=1}^{b}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>s</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>b</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{S^{b}}=h_{i}^{s}=\{f_{I}(s_{i})\}_{i=1}^{b},</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.8" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <math id="alg1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H^{T^{b}}=h_{i}^{t}=\{f_{T}(t_{i})\}_{i=1}^{b};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>b</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H^{T^{b}}=h_{i}^{t}=\{f_{T}(t_{i})\}_{i=1}^{b};</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.9" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Create a <math id="alg1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b\times b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b\times b</annotation></semantics></math> matrix with diagonal values set to <math id="alg1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1/b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/b</annotation></semantics></math>:
<math id="alg1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\pi}=\operatorname{eye}(b,\frac{1}{b});" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>eye</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>,</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>b</mi></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\pi}=\operatorname{eye}(b,\frac{1}{b});</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.10" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Calculate the Mahalanobis distance matrix within the mini-batch: 
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.11" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  (a) between volume and slice embeddings: 
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.12" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="alg1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{M}^{\hat{H}^{X^{b}},H^{S^{b}}}=\sqrt{(\hat{H}^{X^{b}}-H^{S^{b}})^{\top}M(\hat{H}^{X^{b}}-H^{S^{b}})}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{M}^{\hat{H}^{X^{b}},H^{S^{b}}}=\sqrt{(\hat{H}^{X^{b}}-H^{S^{b}})^{\top}M(\hat{H}^{X^{b}}-H^{S^{b}})},</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.13" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  (b) between volume and text embeddings: 
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.14" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> <math id="alg1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C_{M}^{\hat{H}^{X^{b}},H^{T^{b}}}=\sqrt{(\hat{H}^{X^{b}}-H^{T^{b}})^{\top}M(\hat{H}^{X^{b}}-H^{T^{b}})};" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>⊤</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msqrt></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C_{M}^{\hat{H}^{X^{b}},H^{T^{b}}}=\sqrt{(\hat{H}^{X^{b}}-H^{T^{b}})^{\top}M(\hat{H}^{X^{b}}-H^{T^{b}})};</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.15" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Compute the transportation plans using Bregman <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>]</cite> to match volume with slice and text, respectively:
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.16" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <math id="alg1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{s,\epsilon,C(\theta,\psi,M)}^{X^{b},S^{b}}=\operatorname{Bregman}(s,C_{M}^{\hat{H}^{X^{b}},H^{S^{b}}},\epsilon)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Bregman</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{s,\epsilon,C(\theta,\psi,M)}^{X^{b},S^{b}}=\operatorname{Bregman}(s,C_{M}^{\hat{H}^{X^{b}},H^{S^{b}}},\epsilon),</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.17" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <math id="alg1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{s,\epsilon,C(\theta,\psi,M)}^{X^{b},T^{b}}=\operatorname{Bregman}(s,C_{M}^{\hat{H}^{X^{b}},H^{T^{b}}},\epsilon);" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Bregman</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>M</mi><mrow><msup><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{s,\epsilon,C(\theta,\psi,M)}^{X^{b},T^{b}}=\operatorname{Bregman}(s,C_{M}^{\hat{H}^{X^{b}},H^{T^{b}}},\epsilon);</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.18" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Estimate the mPOT loss function in Eq. <a href="#S3.E2" title="In III-D1 Alignment Loss ‣ III-D Learning Objectives ‣ III Methodology ‣ Enhancing 3D Medical Image Understanding with Pretraining Aided by 2D Multimodal Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>: 
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.19" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <math id="alg1.m25" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta,\psi,M)=KL(\hat{\pi}\|\pi_{s,\epsilon,C(\theta,\psi,M)}^{X^{b},S^{b}})+KL(\hat{\pi}\|\pi_{s,\epsilon,C(\theta,\psi,M)}^{X^{b},T^{b}});" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>L</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>π</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∥</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>T</mi><mi>b</mi></msup></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta,\psi,M)=KL(\hat{\pi}\|\pi_{s,\epsilon,C(\theta,\psi,M)}^{X^{b},S^{b}})+KL(\hat{\pi}\|\pi_{s,\epsilon,C(\theta,\psi,M)}^{X^{b},T^{b}});</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.20" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span> Update the learnable parameters: 
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.21" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <math id="alg1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta=\theta-\alpha\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta=\theta-\alpha\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L},</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.22" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <math id="alg1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\psi=\psi-\alpha\nabla_{\psi}\mathcal{L}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ψ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi>ψ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi=\psi-\alpha\nabla_{\psi}\mathcal{L},</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.23" class="ltx_listingline"> <span class="ltx_rule" style="width:1px;height:100%;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>  <math id="alg1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=\operatorname{Proj}(M-\alpha\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>Proj</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>M</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=\operatorname{Proj}(M-\alpha\nabla_{M}\mathcal{L}).</annotation></semantics></math> 
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.24" class="ltx_listingline">end while
</div>
<div id="alg1.3.25" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="alg1.3.25.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <em id="alg1.3.25.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic"><math id="alg1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{V(\theta)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{V(\theta)}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{Proj(\psi)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{Proj(\psi)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="alg1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math></em>

</div>
</div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>Learning mini-batch partial optimal transport (mPOT) cost metric using Mahanalobis distance.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS4.SSS2.6" class="ltx_text">III-D</span>2 </span>Reconstruction Loss</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To enhance visual feature learning with low-level visual semantics, we introduce an auxiliary pretext task, image restoration, which aims to reconstruct the 3D medical volume using latent visual encoders. The training objective for this task is formulated using the L2-norm below:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{res}}=\mathbb{E}_{(X^{b})}\sum_{i=1}^{b}||x_{i}-\hat{x}_{i}||_{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>res</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>b</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false" rspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>b</mi></munderover><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>x</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{res}}=\mathbb{E}_{(X^{b})}\sum_{i=1}^{b}||x_{i}-\hat{x}_{i}||_{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S3.SS4.SSS3.6" class="ltx_text">III-D</span>3 </span>Total Loss</h4>

<div id="S3.SS4.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">With a synergy of triplet alignment learning and image reconstruction, the overall pretraining objective of our Med3DInsight framework is represented as follows:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{mPOT}}+\mathcal{L}_{\text{res}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>total</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>mPOT</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>res</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\text{total}}=\mathcal{L}_{\text{mPOT}}+\mathcal{L}_{\text{res}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS4.SSS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">After pretraining, the resulting 3D image encoder serves as a visual backbone for downstream task-specific fine-tuning.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">IV </span><span id="S4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiments</span></h2>

<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS1.6" class="ltx_text">IV-A</span> </span><span id="S4.SS1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Pretraining Setup and Settings</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Med3DInsight, the nnFormer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>]</cite> serves as the 3D visual encoder, and the CLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> serves as the 2D image and text encoder.
We freeze the image and text encoder during pretraining to optimize computational efficiency while extracting pre-aligned embeddings from image slices and texts with CLIP, compelling the 3D visual backbone to learn more representative features.
However, since the image and generated descriptions are in the medical domain while CLIP has been pre-trained on natural image-text pairs, we fine-tune the CLIP encoders using our generated 2D image-text pairs before our pretraining.
For the PSAT module, we follow a common setting and set the token number of learnable queries <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q</annotation></semantics></math> to 300, and the dimension of the token is 512.
During pretraining, we employ a batch size of 4 on each GPU and utilize the Adam optimizer with an initial learning rate of 2e-5. The training is set for 300 epochs to ensure convergence. All pretraining experiments are implemented on four NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090 GPUs.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S4.SS2.6" class="ltx_text">IV-B</span> </span><span id="S4.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Downstream Tasks and Corresponding Datasets</span></h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate the effectiveness of our method across a diverse set of 3D medical imaging tasks, varying in size, objectives, and modalities.
Specifically, we evaluate the representativity and adaptability of the visual features informed by MLLMs.
These evaluations span four types of distinct 3D medical image segmentation tasks and two 3D medical image classification task, covering over 19 organs or tumors, and spanning both CT and MRI modalities, as listed in Table <a href="#S4.T3" title="TABLE III ‣ IV-B Downstream Tasks and Corresponding Datasets ‣ IV Experiments ‣ Enhancing 3D Medical Image Understanding with Pretraining Aided by 2D Multimodal Large Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">III</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T3" class="ltx_table">
